    "text": "13 \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nNp=08<br>Bernoulli Box−PF  Np=16<br>1 Np=20<br>Np=32<br>0.5 Np=44<br>Np=52<br>0<br>0 10 20 30 40 50 60<br>Discrete−time k<br>Np=08<br>Np=16<br>1 Np=20<br>Np=32<br>0.5 Np=44<br>Np=52<br>0<br>0 10 20 30 40 50 60<br>Discrete−time k<br>Np=08<br>Np=16<br>150 Np=20<br>100 Np=32<br>Np=44<br>50 Np=52<br>0<br>0 10 20 30 40 50 60<br>Discrete−time k<br>prob. exist. q k|k<br>inclusion ρk<br>spread νk<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nBernoulli BPF  (n0 =1)<br>22<br>21<br>20<br>19<br>18<br>17<br>16<br>15<br>14<br>13<br>12<br>0 5 10 15 20 25 30 35 40 45<br>Number of particles<br>Computation time for 60 scan [s]<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\nFigure 9. Computational time over M = 100 runs for the Bernoulli Box-PF an a function of the number of particles N and using n0 = 1. \n\nwhere \\ denotes the set-minus operation and κ(Υ) is the pdf of the false alarm random finite set Υ. Under the assumption made in Sec.II the false alarm set is a Poisson RFS, whose multi-object pdf is given by [10, p.366]: κ(Υ) = e<sup>−λ �</sup> [z]∈Υ<sup>λ c([z]).Thenitfollows</sup> that \n\nFigure 7. Average performance over M = 100 Monte Carlo runs for the Bernoulli Box-PF using n0 = 1 and N ∈{8, 16, 20, 32, 44, 52} box particles: on the top the probability of existence qk|k, in the middle the inclusion ρk, at the bottom volume (spread) νk. \n\n\n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nBernoulli PF<br>60<br>n0=0500<br>n0=1000<br>n0=5000<br>50<br>40<br>30<br>20<br>10<br>0<br>0 1000 2000 3000 4000 5000<br>Number of particles<br>Computation time for 60 scan [s]<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\nwhich leads to the update equations in the form given by eqs.(7)-(9). \n\n_b) Constraints propagation algorithm.:_ The CP algorithm [26] that was used in the numerical example in which the measurements are intervals in the range, range-rate, azimuth space, is presented in Algorithm 3. This algorithm performs the contraction of each box particle at the update step. \n\n**Acknowledgements.** A. Gning and L. Mihaylova acknowledge the support of the EPSRC project EP/E027253/1 and all the authors are thankful to the [European Community’s] Seventh Framework Programme [FP7/2007-2013] under grant agreement No 238710 (Monte Carlo based Innovative Management and Processing for an Unrivalled Leap in Sensor Exploitation). \n\nFigure 8. Computational time averaged over M = 100 runs for the Bernoulli PF an a function of the number of particles N . We show the results for n0 = 500, 1000, 5000. \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nqk+1|k+1 =<br>κ(Υk+1\\{[z]})<br>1−pD +pD �[z]∈Υk+1 � gk+1([z]|x) sk+1|k (x) dx κ(Υk+1)<br>(49)<br>κ(Υk+1\\{[z]})<br>qk − +1 1 |k −pD+pD �[z]∈Υk+1 � gk+1([z]|x) sk+1|k(x) dx κ(Υk+1)<br>sk+1|k+1(x) = [1]<br>κ(Υk+1\\{[z]})<br>1−pD +pD � [z]∈Υk+1gk+1([z]|x)<br>κ(Υk+1)<br>sk+1|k(x) (50)<br>κ(Υk+1 \\{[z]})<br>1−pD +pD �[z]∈Υk+1 � gk+1([z]|x) sk+1|k (x) dx κ(Υk+1)<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n# REFERENCES (49) \n\n[1] A. H. Jazwinski, _Stochastic Processes and Filtering Theory_ . Academic \n\nPress, 1970. \n\n"
